    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures how Hessian-regularizing training methods relate to train/test performance and the generalization gap, using a different measure and setting from the listed versions.",
    "gate_note": "Own results compare SGD, WP-SGD, and NSO on natural-label classification tasks. Figure 3 reports generalization-gap curves; Table 2 reports train and test accuracy. The claim versions do not cover this study’s noise-injection methods or pretrained fine-tuning setting.",
    "test_reason": "Section 3.1.3 and Figure 5 test V5 in a new setting: pretrained ResNet-34 fine-tuning on Indoor and Aircraft with SAM and NSO, varying batch size from 8 to 64 while holding epochs fixed. The text identifies batch size 32 as best. The extracted rows themselves test none of the listed versions.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The paraphrase broadly captures the authors' conclusion: ordinary weight perturbation provides limited benefits, whereas two-point NSO regularizes the Hessian and improves performance in fine-tuning, CLIP pretraining, and chain-of-thought tuning. Qualify any suggestion of universal improvement: NSO does not beat SGD on Caltech-256 in Table 3, and Binomial noise performs poorly in Table 2. The authors describe their bound as a data-dependent, descriptive explanation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Fine-tuning pretrained models and pretraining with weight-noise methods; the study does not test random-label memorization, width sweeps, or the version-specific interventions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Noise distribution and variance; learning rate and epoch count; batch size; SAM perturbation radius; combination with data augmentation or distance-based regularization. The text reports varying outcomes for some of these settings.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Gaussian NSO improves Table 2 test accuracy over SGD by 2.5, 1.4, and 4.9 percentage points on Aircraft, Indoor, and Retina, respectively. Training accuracy decreases on the first two tasks. Figure 3 reports lower loss gaps during NSO training.",
        "proposed_claim": "Two-point weight-noise training can improve fine-tuning performance and reduce loss gaps relative to SGD and ordinary weight perturbation.",
